{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01HkqYSaYpkHJzvura44evhP","type":"tool_result","content":"1\tAll source material is gathered. One blocker to report first.\n2\t\n3\t## Blocker: this session has no filesystem write access\n4\t\n5\t`Write` is denied for every path, and sandboxed Bash refuses output redirection even into `C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp` itself. That matches `CLAUDE.md`'s note that the executor removes `Write` from `--allowed-tools` so stage output flows through stdout — which works for a single-artifact stage, but this stage produces six files.\n6\t\n7\tThe research itself is **complete**: all four blocking gaps from § Open questions #2 are closed, plus the sidecar page for #3. Everything below is finished content, ready to write verbatim. The orchestrator captures stdout into `research-manifest.md`, so the manifest lands correctly; the five snapshots need a materialization pass.\n8\t\n9\t---\n10\t\n11\t# Research Manifest — KCNA Chapter 5\n12\t\n13\t## Snapshots fetched\n14\t\n15\t### New this stage (pending write to `../Book-KCNA/sources/`)\n16\t\n17\t| Snapshot | Authority | Objectives | Concepts |\n18\t|---|---|---|---|\n19\t| `k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | pod · smallest-deployable-unit · co-located-co-scheduled · pod-shared-context · pod-network-namespace · pod-ip · localhost-communication · pod-shared-volumes · podspec · pod-template · single-container-pod · multi-container-pod · pod-ephemerality · pod-replacement |\n20\t| `k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | init-container · init-container-ordering · run-to-completion · restart-policy · phase-pending |\n21\t| `k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | qos-class · qos-guaranteed · qos-burstable · qos-besteffort · pod-eviction · resource-request · resource-limit |\n22\t| `k8s-docs-sidecar-containers-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | sidecar-container · multi-container-pod · init-container · probe |\n23\t| `k8s-docs-pod-termination-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | pod-termination · pod-lifetime · pod-phase · restart-policy |\n24\t\n25\t### Already cached and verified against this chapter's needs\n26\t\n27\t| Snapshot | Covers |\n28\t|---|---|\n29\t| `k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23.md` | §5 phases/states/`restartPolicy`/backoff · §7 probes — **fully sourced, verified this stage** |\n30\t| `k8s-docs-resource-management-2026-08-23.md` | §8 movements 1–3 including the `m`/`M` warning — verified |\n31\t| `k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23.md` | §6 all four facts + TokenRequest — **G7 confirmed closed** |\n32\t| `k8s-docs-network-model-2026-08-23.md` | §1 unique cluster-wide IP, private namespace, `localhost` — verified |\n33\t| `k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23.md` | §1 co-located/co-scheduled · §4 immutability parallel — verified |\n34\t| `k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23.md` | §1 Pod definition · §4 controllers create replacements — verified |\n35\t| `k8s-docs-names-and-uids-2026-08-24.md` | §4 UID distinguishes historical occurrences — verified |\n36\t| `k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23.md` | §5 `ImagePullBackOff` + `imagePullPolicy` (Ch 2 pinned payoff) — verified |\n37\t| `k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23.md` | §6 `kubernetes.io/service-account-token` (Ch 4 pinned payoff) — verified |\n38\t| `k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23.md` | `kubectl-get` / `-describe` / `-explain` — verified |\n39\t\n40\t## Gaps\n41\t\n42\t**None blocking.** Every concept in `kb_tags` now resolves to an authoritative source. G3 is closed (QoS half retrieved); the Pod concept page, init containers, and the pod-lifecycle termination tail are all retrieved.\n43\t\n44\tTwo minor items, neither load-bearing:\n45\t\n46\t- **`status.qosClass`** — the QoS page does not name this field in its prose. Do not assert the field name in the draft; say the class is assigned, not where it is reported.\n47\t- **`kubectl explain` detail** — only the one-line operation description is cached. Sufficient for the passing mention `kb_tags` implies; insufficient if drafting wants a worked invocation.\n48\t\n49\t## Notes for the author\n50\t\n51\t**1. Three open questions are now answerable.**\n52\t\n53\t- **#3 (sidecar implementation)** — the source establishes it plainly: *\"Kubernetes implements sidecar containers as a special case of init containers\"*, active by default since v1.29, enabled via `restartPolicy: Always` on an `initContainers` entry. Your recommendation's condition is met. It also connects §2 and §3 exactly as you predicted, and the sidecar page carries the ordering guarantee and reverse-order termination.\n54\t- **#5 (graceful termination)** — now fully sourced: default grace period 30 seconds, `preStop` hook, TERM then KILL, `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, and the 2-second one-off extension. Your proposed altitude — *\"termination is a request with a deadline, not an instant event\"* — is directly supportable.\n55\t- **#7 (init resource accounting)** — documented on **both** the init-containers and sidecar pages, in plain terms. It is less obscure than the outline assumed. Still your call whether §8 can absorb it.\n56\t\n57\t**2. One correction the draft should not overstate.** §3's plan says a failed init container is restarted \"according to the Pod's `restartPolicy`.\" The source adds a wrinkle: *\"if the Pod `restartPolicy` is set to Always, the init containers use `restartPolicy` OnFailure.\"* The `Never` → Pod-failed behavior is exactly as planned; the `Always` case is not a straight inheritance.\n58\t\n59\t**3. §7's \"init containers don't carry probes\" needs the sidecar exception.** Sourced as *\"Regular init containers ... do not support the `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` fields\"* — the word *regular* is load-bearing, because sidecars support all three. If §2 names the modern implementation (#3), §7's axis needs the qualifier.\n60\t\n61\t**4. QoS criteria carry clauses the outline didn't anticipate.** Burstable and BestEffort now both reference *\"or the Pod has a Pod-level memory or CPU request or limit\"* — Pod-level resources, a newer feature. The three-class taxonomy is unaffected and associate-tier framing holds; just don't quote the criteria as a clean two-bullet list. Also useful for `ch05-fig05`: the QoS class is fixed at creation and immutable for the Pod's lifetime.\n62\t\n63\t**5. Version drift in the docs.** The Pods page reads \"in Kubernetes v1.36.\" The KCNA curriculum is effective 2025-11-24. Nothing in Chapter 5's material is version-sensitive at associate tier, but the Pod conditions list now includes `PodReadyToStartContainers` (beta), `DisruptionTarget`, `PodResizePending`, and `PodResizeInProgress` — all almost certainly out of scope, and worth holding out alongside your § Open questions #11 list.\n64\t\n65\t**6. Termination has an EndpointSlice entanglement.** The termination section states the control plane evaluates removal from EndpointSlice objects concurrently with shutdown. If #5 goes in, §4 must not develop this — it collides with your §7 instruction not to teach EndpointSlice, and Ch 9 owns the object.\n66\t\n67\t**7. Provenance note on two fetches.** `k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md` and `k8s-docs-pod-termination-2026-08-24.md` were retrieved from the `kubernetes/website` repository's raw markdown, because the rendered pages truncated mid-document. Same document, same authority, canonical URL recorded in `source_url` with the raw path in `source_url_raw`. `k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md` is a merge of both retrievals for the same reason.\n68\t\n69\t---\n70\t\n71\t## Files to write\n72\t\n73\tEach block below is a complete file. Path first, content between the markers.\n74\t\n75\t<details>"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72aeec98-88aa-4709-9e4d-adb660dd6897","uuid":"081f1db1-1abd-45cf-91ce-b17b26de5e29","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:44:41.160Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-05\\research-manifest.md","content":"All source material is gathered. One blocker to report first.\n\n## Blocker: this session has no filesystem write access\n\n`Write` is denied for every path, and sandboxed Bash refuses output redirection even into `C:\\dev\\lodestar\\certcomp` itself. That matches `CLAUDE.md`'s note that the executor removes `Write` from `--allowed-tools` so stage output flows through stdout — which works for a single-artifact stage, but this stage produces six files.\n\nThe research itself is **complete**: all four blocking gaps from § Open questions #2 are closed, plus the sidecar page for #3. Everything below is finished content, ready to write verbatim. The orchestrator captures stdout into `research-manifest.md`, so the manifest lands correctly; the five snapshots need a materialization pass.\n\n---\n\n# Research Manifest — KCNA Chapter 5\n\n## Snapshots fetched\n\n### New this stage (pending write to `../Book-KCNA/sources/`)\n\n| Snapshot | Authority | Objectives | Concepts |\n|---|---|---|---|\n| `k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | pod · smallest-deployable-unit · co-located-co-scheduled · pod-shared-context · pod-network-namespace · pod-ip · localhost-communication · pod-shared-volumes · podspec · pod-template · single-container-pod · multi-container-pod · pod-ephemerality · pod-replacement |\n| `k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | init-container · init-container-ordering · run-to-completion · restart-policy · phase-pending |\n| `k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | qos-class · qos-guaranteed · qos-burstable · qos-besteffort · pod-eviction · resource-request · resource-limit |\n| `k8s-docs-sidecar-containers-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | sidecar-container · multi-container-pod · init-container · probe |\n| `k8s-docs-pod-termination-2026-08-24.md` | Kubernetes project | D1.1 | pod-termination · pod-lifetime · pod-phase · restart-policy |\n\n### Already cached and verified against this chapter's needs\n\n| Snapshot | Covers |\n|---|---|\n| `k8s-docs-pod-lifecycle-2026-08-23.md` | §5 phases/states/`restartPolicy`/backoff · §7 probes — **fully sourced, verified this stage** |\n| `k8s-docs-resource-management-2026-08-23.md` | §8 movements 1–3 including the `m`/`M` warning — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-service-accounts-2026-08-23.md` | §6 all four facts + TokenRequest — **G7 confirmed closed** |\n| `k8s-docs-network-model-2026-08-23.md` | §1 unique cluster-wide IP, private namespace, `localhost` — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-containers-2026-08-23.md` | §1 co-located/co-scheduled · §4 immutability parallel — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-workloads-2026-08-23.md` | §1 Pod definition · §4 controllers create replacements — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-names-and-uids-2026-08-24.md` | §4 UID distinguishes historical occurrences — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-images-2026-08-23.md` | §5 `ImagePullBackOff` + `imagePullPolicy` (Ch 2 pinned payoff) — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-secret-2026-08-23.md` | §6 `kubernetes.io/service-account-token` (Ch 4 pinned payoff) — verified |\n| `k8s-docs-kubectl-overview-2026-08-23.md` | `kubectl-get` / `-describe` / `-explain` — verified |\n\n## Gaps\n\n**None blocking.** Every concept in `kb_tags` now resolves to an authoritative source. G3 is closed (QoS half retrieved); the Pod concept page, init containers, and the pod-lifecycle termination tail are all retrieved.\n\nTwo minor items, neither load-bearing:\n\n- **`status.qosClass`** — the QoS page does not name this field in its prose. Do not assert the field name in the draft; say the class is assigned, not where it is reported.\n- **`kubectl explain` detail** — only the one-line operation description is cached. Sufficient for the passing mention `kb_tags` implies; insufficient if drafting wants a worked invocation.\n\n## Notes for the author\n\n**1. Three open questions are now answerable.**\n\n- **#3 (sidecar implementation)** — the source establishes it plainly: *\"Kubernetes implements sidecar containers as a special case of init containers\"*, active by default since v1.29, enabled via `restartPolicy: Always` on an `initContainers` entry. Your recommendation's condition is met. It also connects §2 and §3 exactly as you predicted, and the sidecar page carries the ordering guarantee and reverse-order termination.\n- **#5 (graceful termination)** — now fully sourced: default grace period 30 seconds, `preStop` hook, TERM then KILL, `terminationGracePeriodSeconds`, and the 2-second one-off extension. Your proposed altitude — *\"termination is a request with a deadline, not an instant event\"* — is directly supportable.\n- **#7 (init resource accounting)** — documented on **both** the init-containers and sidecar pages, in plain terms. It is less obscure than the outline assumed. Still your call whether §8 can absorb it.\n\n**2. One correction the draft should not overstate.** §3's plan says a failed init container is restarted \"according to the Pod's `restartPolicy`.\" The source adds a wrinkle: *\"if the Pod `restartPolicy` is set to Always, the init containers use `restartPolicy` OnFailure.\"* The `Never` → Pod-failed behavior is exactly as planned; the `Always` case is not a straight inheritance.\n\n**3. §7's \"init containers don't carry probes\" needs the sidecar exception.** Sourced as *\"Regular init containers ... do not support the `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` fields\"* — the word *regular* is load-bearing, because sidecars support all three. If §2 names the modern implementation (#3), §7's axis needs the qualifier.\n\n**4. QoS criteria carry clauses the outline didn't anticipate.** Burstable and BestEffort now both reference *\"or the Pod has a Pod-level memory or CPU request or limit\"* — Pod-level resources, a newer feature. The three-class taxonomy is unaffected and associate-tier framing holds; just don't quote the criteria as a clean two-bullet list. Also useful for `ch05-fig05`: the QoS class is fixed at creation and immutable for the Pod's lifetime.\n\n**5. Version drift in the docs.** The Pods page reads \"in Kubernetes v1.36.\" The KCNA curriculum is effective 2025-11-24. Nothing in Chapter 5's material is version-sensitive at associate tier, but the Pod conditions list now includes `PodReadyToStartContainers` (beta), `DisruptionTarget`, `PodResizePending`, and `PodResizeInProgress` — all almost certainly out of scope, and worth holding out alongside your § Open questions #11 list.\n\n**6. Termination has an EndpointSlice entanglement.** The termination section states the control plane evaluates removal from EndpointSlice objects concurrently with shutdown. If #5 goes in, §4 must not develop this — it collides with your §7 instruction not to teach EndpointSlice, and Ch 9 owns the object.\n\n**7. Provenance note on two fetches.** `k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md` and `k8s-docs-pod-termination-2026-08-24.md` were retrieved from the `kubernetes/website` repository's raw markdown, because the rendered pages truncated mid-document. Same document, same authority, canonical URL recorded in `source_url` with the raw path in `source_url_raw`. `k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md` is a merge of both retrievals for the same reason.\n\n---\n\n## Files to write\n\nEach block below is a complete file. Path first, content between the markers.\n\n<details>","numLines":75,"startLine":1,"totalLines":555}}}
