{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01FQPysnbsyZokPGtiD27C6H","type":"tool_result","content":"75\t<details>\n76\t<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n77\t\n78\t```markdown\n79\t---\n80\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/\"\n81\tsource_url_raw: \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/_index.md\"\n82\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T06:20:00-0400\"\n83\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\n84\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1.1\"]\n85\tconcepts_covered: [\"pod\", \"smallest-deployable-unit\", \"co-located-co-scheduled\", \"pod-shared-context\", \"pod-network-namespace\", \"pod-ip\", \"localhost-communication\", \"pod-shared-volumes\", \"podspec\", \"pod-template\", \"single-container-pod\", \"multi-container-pod\", \"sidecar-container\", \"pod-ephemerality\", \"pod-replacement\", \"pod-lifetime\"]\n86\t---\n87\t# Pods (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/)\n88\t\n89\t## Opening definition\n90\t\n91\t*Pods* are the smallest deployable units of computing that you can create and manage in Kubernetes.\n92\t\n93\tA *Pod* (as in a pod of whales or pea pod) is a group of one or more containers, with shared storage and network resources, and a specification for how to run the containers. A Pod's contents are always co-located and co-scheduled, and run in a shared context. A Pod models an application-specific \"logical host\": it contains one or more application containers which are relatively tightly coupled. In non-cloud contexts, applications executed on the same physical or virtual machine are analogous to cloud applications executed on the same logical host.\n94\t\n95\tAs well as application containers, a Pod can contain init containers that run during Pod startup. You can also inject ephemeral containers for debugging a running Pod.\n96\t\n97\t## What is a Pod?\n98\t\n99\tNote: You need to install a container runtime into each node in the cluster so that Pods can run there.\n100\t\n101\tThe shared context of a Pod is a set of Linux namespaces, cgroups, and potentially other facets of isolation - the same things that isolate a container. Within a Pod's context, the individual applications may have further sub-isolations applied.\n102\t\n103\tA Pod is similar to a set of containers with shared namespaces and shared filesystem volumes.\n104\t\n105\t## Pods with multiple containers\n106\t\n107\tPods are designed to support multiple cooperating processes (as containers) that form a cohesive unit of service. The containers in a Pod are automatically co-located and co-scheduled on the same physical or virtual machine in the cluster. The containers can share resources and dependencies, communicate with one another, and coordinate when and how they are terminated.\n108\t\n109\tPods in a Kubernetes cluster are used in two main ways:\n110\t\n111\t* **Pods that run a single container**. The \"one-container-per-Pod\" model is the most common Kubernetes use case; in this case, you can think of a Pod as a wrapper around a single container; Kubernetes manages Pods rather than managing the containers directly.\n112\t* **Pods that run multiple containers that need to work together**. A Pod can encapsulate an application composed of multiple co-located containers that are tightly coupled and need to share resources. These co-located containers form a single cohesive unit of service—for example, one container serving data stored in a shared volume to the public, while a separate sidecar container refreshes or updates those files. The Pod wraps these containers, storage resources, and an ephemeral network identity together as a single unit.\n113\t\n114\t    Grouping multiple co-located and co-managed containers in a single Pod is a relatively advanced use case. You should use this pattern only in specific instances in which your containers are tightly coupled.\n115\t\n116\t    You don't need to run multiple containers to provide replication (for resilience or capacity); if you need multiple replicas, see Workload management.\n117\t\n118\t## Using Pods\n119\t\n120\tThe following is an example of a Pod which consists of a container running the image `nginx:1.14.2`.\n121\t\n122\t    apiVersion: v1\n123\t    kind: Pod\n124\t    metadata:\n125\t      name: nginx\n126\t    spec:\n127\t      containers:\n128\t      - name: nginx\n129\t        image: nginx:1.14.2"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72aeec98-88aa-4709-9e4d-adb660dd6897","uuid":"4db50f56-535b-4534-ace2-dd27d9edf26d","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:44:54.501Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-05\\research-manifest.md","content":"<details>\n<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-pods-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n\n```markdown\n---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/\"\nsource_url_raw: \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/_index.md\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T06:20:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1.1\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"pod\", \"smallest-deployable-unit\", \"co-located-co-scheduled\", \"pod-shared-context\", \"pod-network-namespace\", \"pod-ip\", \"localhost-communication\", \"pod-shared-volumes\", \"podspec\", \"pod-template\", \"single-container-pod\", \"multi-container-pod\", \"sidecar-container\", \"pod-ephemerality\", \"pod-replacement\", \"pod-lifetime\"]\n---\n# Pods (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/)\n\n## Opening definition\n\n*Pods* are the smallest deployable units of computing that you can create and manage in Kubernetes.\n\nA *Pod* (as in a pod of whales or pea pod) is a group of one or more containers, with shared storage and network resources, and a specification for how to run the containers. A Pod's contents are always co-located and co-scheduled, and run in a shared context. A Pod models an application-specific \"logical host\": it contains one or more application containers which are relatively tightly coupled. In non-cloud contexts, applications executed on the same physical or virtual machine are analogous to cloud applications executed on the same logical host.\n\nAs well as application containers, a Pod can contain init containers that run during Pod startup. You can also inject ephemeral containers for debugging a running Pod.\n\n## What is a Pod?\n\nNote: You need to install a container runtime into each node in the cluster so that Pods can run there.\n\nThe shared context of a Pod is a set of Linux namespaces, cgroups, and potentially other facets of isolation - the same things that isolate a container. Within a Pod's context, the individual applications may have further sub-isolations applied.\n\nA Pod is similar to a set of containers with shared namespaces and shared filesystem volumes.\n\n## Pods with multiple containers\n\nPods are designed to support multiple cooperating processes (as containers) that form a cohesive unit of service. The containers in a Pod are automatically co-located and co-scheduled on the same physical or virtual machine in the cluster. The containers can share resources and dependencies, communicate with one another, and coordinate when and how they are terminated.\n\nPods in a Kubernetes cluster are used in two main ways:\n\n* **Pods that run a single container**. The \"one-container-per-Pod\" model is the most common Kubernetes use case; in this case, you can think of a Pod as a wrapper around a single container; Kubernetes manages Pods rather than managing the containers directly.\n* **Pods that run multiple containers that need to work together**. A Pod can encapsulate an application composed of multiple co-located containers that are tightly coupled and need to share resources. These co-located containers form a single cohesive unit of service—for example, one container serving data stored in a shared volume to the public, while a separate sidecar container refreshes or updates those files. The Pod wraps these containers, storage resources, and an ephemeral network identity together as a single unit.\n\n    Grouping multiple co-located and co-managed containers in a single Pod is a relatively advanced use case. You should use this pattern only in specific instances in which your containers are tightly coupled.\n\n    You don't need to run multiple containers to provide replication (for resilience or capacity); if you need multiple replicas, see Workload management.\n\n## Using Pods\n\nThe following is an example of a Pod which consists of a container running the image `nginx:1.14.2`.\n\n    apiVersion: v1\n    kind: Pod\n    metadata:\n      name: nginx\n    spec:\n      containers:\n      - name: nginx\n        image: nginx:1.14.2","numLines":55,"startLine":75,"totalLines":555}}}
