{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01QAWjyhs55oVvaAn37y2F61","type":"tool_result","content":"210\t<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n211\t\n212\t```markdown\n213\t---\n214\tsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/\"\n215\tsource_url_raw: \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers.md\"\n216\tfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T06:20:00-0400\"\n217\tauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\n218\tobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1.1\"]\n219\tconcepts_covered: [\"init-container\", \"init-container-ordering\", \"run-to-completion\", \"restart-policy\", \"phase-pending\", \"sidecar-container\"]\n220\t---\n221\t# Init Containers (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/)\n222\t\n223\t## Overview\n224\t\n225\tThis page provides an overview of init containers: specialized containers that run before app containers in a Pod. Init containers can contain utilities or setup scripts not present in an app image.\n226\t\n227\t## Understanding init containers\n228\t\n229\tA Pod can have multiple containers running apps within it, but it can also have one or more init containers, which are run before the app containers are started.\n230\t\n231\tInit containers are exactly like regular containers, except:\n232\t\n233\t* Init containers always run to completion.\n234\t* Each init container must complete successfully before the next one starts.\n235\t\n236\tIf a Pod's init container fails, the kubelet repeatedly restarts that init container until it succeeds. However, if the Pod has a `restartPolicy` of Never, and an init container fails during startup of that Pod, Kubernetes treats the overall Pod as failed.\n237\t\n238\t## Differences from regular containers\n239\t\n240\tInit containers support all the fields and features of app containers, including resource limits, volumes, and security settings. However, the resource requests and limits for an init container are handled differently, as documented in Resource sharing within containers.\n241\t\n242\tRegular init containers (in other words: excluding sidecar containers) do not support the `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` fields. Init containers must run to completion before the Pod can be ready; sidecar containers continue running during a Pod's lifetime, and *do* support some probes.\n243\t\n244\t## Differences from sidecar containers\n245\t\n246\tInit containers run and complete their tasks before the main application container starts. Unlike sidecar containers, init containers are not continuously running alongside the main containers.\n247\t\n248\tInit containers run to completion sequentially, and the main container does not start until all the init containers have successfully completed.\n249\t\n250\tinit containers do not support `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` whereas sidecar containers support all these probes to control their lifecycle.\n251\t\n252\tInit containers share the same resources (CPU, memory, network) with the main application containers but do not interact directly with them. They can, however, use shared volumes for data exchange.\n253\t\n254\t## Using init containers\n255\t\n256\tBecause init containers have separate images from app containers, they have some advantages for start-up related code:\n257\t\n258\t* Init containers can contain utilities or custom code for setup that are not present in an app image. For example, there is no need to make an image `FROM` another image just to use a tool like `sed`, `awk`, `python`, or `dig` during setup.\n259\t* The application image builder and deployer roles can work independently without the need to jointly build a single app image.\n260\t* Init containers can run with a different view of the filesystem than app containers in the same Pod. Consequently, they can be given access to Secrets that app containers cannot access.\n261\t* Because init containers run to completion before any app containers start, init containers offer a mechanism to block or delay app container startup until a set of preconditions are met. Once preconditions are met, all of the app containers in a Pod can start in parallel.\n262\t* Init containers can securely run utilities or custom code that would otherwise make an app container image less secure. By keeping unnecessary tools separate you can limit the attack surface of your app container image.\n263\t\n264\t## Detailed behavior\n265\t\n266\tDuring Pod startup, the kubelet delays running init containers until the networking and storage are ready. Then the kubelet runs the Pod's init containers in the order they appear in the Pod's spec.\n267\t\n268\tEach init container must exit successfully before the next container starts. If a container fails to start due to the runtime or exits with failure, it is retried according to the Pod `restartPolicy`. However, if the Pod `restartPolicy` is set to Always, the init containers use `restartPolicy` OnFailure.\n269\t\n270\tA Pod cannot be `Ready` until all init containers have succeeded. The ports on an init container are not aggregated under a Service. A Pod that is initializing is in the `Pending` state but should have a condition `Initialized` set to false.\n271\t\n272\tIf the Pod restarts, or is restarted, all init containers must execute again.\n273\t\n274\t## Resource sharing within containers\n275\t\n276\tThe highest of any particular resource request or limit defined on all init containers is the *effective init request/limit*. If any resource has no resource limit specified this is considered as the highest limit.\n277\t\n278\tThe Pod's *effective request/limit* for a resource is the higher of:\n279\t\n280\t* the sum of all app containers request/limit for a resource\n281\t* the effective init request/limit for a resource\n282\t\n283\t## Pod restart reasons\n284\t\n285\tThe Pod infrastructure container is restarted. This is uncommon and would have to be done by someone with root access to nodes.\n286\t\n287\tAll containers in a Pod are terminated while `restartPolicy` is set to Always, forcing a restart, and the init container completion record has been lost due to garbage collection.\n288\t```\n289\t\n290\t</details>\n291\t\n292\t<details>\n293\t<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n294\t"}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"72aeec98-88aa-4709-9e4d-adb660dd6897","uuid":"3a7c3d3d-b672-4a1a-b1aa-683cd2d9c844","timestamp":"2026-08-24T10:45:03.393Z","tool_use_result":{"type":"text","file":{"filePath":"C:\\dev\\lodestar\\Book-KCNA\\.pipeline-state\\ch-05\\research-manifest.md","content":"<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-init-containers-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n\n```markdown\n---\nsource_url: \"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/\"\nsource_url_raw: \"https://raw.githubusercontent.com/kubernetes/website/main/content/en/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers.md\"\nfetched_at: \"2026-08-24T06:20:00-0400\"\nauthority: \"Kubernetes project (kubernetes.io/docs)\"\nobjectives_covered: [\"D1 Core Concepts\", \"D1.1\"]\nconcepts_covered: [\"init-container\", \"init-container-ordering\", \"run-to-completion\", \"restart-policy\", \"phase-pending\", \"sidecar-container\"]\n---\n# Init Containers (kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/workloads/pods/init-containers/)\n\n## Overview\n\nThis page provides an overview of init containers: specialized containers that run before app containers in a Pod. Init containers can contain utilities or setup scripts not present in an app image.\n\n## Understanding init containers\n\nA Pod can have multiple containers running apps within it, but it can also have one or more init containers, which are run before the app containers are started.\n\nInit containers are exactly like regular containers, except:\n\n* Init containers always run to completion.\n* Each init container must complete successfully before the next one starts.\n\nIf a Pod's init container fails, the kubelet repeatedly restarts that init container until it succeeds. However, if the Pod has a `restartPolicy` of Never, and an init container fails during startup of that Pod, Kubernetes treats the overall Pod as failed.\n\n## Differences from regular containers\n\nInit containers support all the fields and features of app containers, including resource limits, volumes, and security settings. However, the resource requests and limits for an init container are handled differently, as documented in Resource sharing within containers.\n\nRegular init containers (in other words: excluding sidecar containers) do not support the `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` fields. Init containers must run to completion before the Pod can be ready; sidecar containers continue running during a Pod's lifetime, and *do* support some probes.\n\n## Differences from sidecar containers\n\nInit containers run and complete their tasks before the main application container starts. Unlike sidecar containers, init containers are not continuously running alongside the main containers.\n\nInit containers run to completion sequentially, and the main container does not start until all the init containers have successfully completed.\n\ninit containers do not support `lifecycle`, `livenessProbe`, `readinessProbe`, or `startupProbe` whereas sidecar containers support all these probes to control their lifecycle.\n\nInit containers share the same resources (CPU, memory, network) with the main application containers but do not interact directly with them. They can, however, use shared volumes for data exchange.\n\n## Using init containers\n\nBecause init containers have separate images from app containers, they have some advantages for start-up related code:\n\n* Init containers can contain utilities or custom code for setup that are not present in an app image. For example, there is no need to make an image `FROM` another image just to use a tool like `sed`, `awk`, `python`, or `dig` during setup.\n* The application image builder and deployer roles can work independently without the need to jointly build a single app image.\n* Init containers can run with a different view of the filesystem than app containers in the same Pod. Consequently, they can be given access to Secrets that app containers cannot access.\n* Because init containers run to completion before any app containers start, init containers offer a mechanism to block or delay app container startup until a set of preconditions are met. Once preconditions are met, all of the app containers in a Pod can start in parallel.\n* Init containers can securely run utilities or custom code that would otherwise make an app container image less secure. By keeping unnecessary tools separate you can limit the attack surface of your app container image.\n\n## Detailed behavior\n\nDuring Pod startup, the kubelet delays running init containers until the networking and storage are ready. Then the kubelet runs the Pod's init containers in the order they appear in the Pod's spec.\n\nEach init container must exit successfully before the next container starts. If a container fails to start due to the runtime or exits with failure, it is retried according to the Pod `restartPolicy`. However, if the Pod `restartPolicy` is set to Always, the init containers use `restartPolicy` OnFailure.\n\nA Pod cannot be `Ready` until all init containers have succeeded. The ports on an init container are not aggregated under a Service. A Pod that is initializing is in the `Pending` state but should have a condition `Initialized` set to false.\n\nIf the Pod restarts, or is restarted, all init containers must execute again.\n\n## Resource sharing within containers\n\nThe highest of any particular resource request or limit defined on all init containers is the *effective init request/limit*. If any resource has no resource limit specified this is considered as the highest limit.\n\nThe Pod's *effective request/limit* for a resource is the higher of:\n\n* the sum of all app containers request/limit for a resource\n* the effective init request/limit for a resource\n\n## Pod restart reasons\n\nThe Pod infrastructure container is restarted. This is uncommon and would have to be done by someone with root access to nodes.\n\nAll containers in a Pod are terminated while `restartPolicy` is set to Always, forcing a restart, and the init container completion record has been lost due to garbage collection.\n```\n\n</details>\n\n<details>\n<summary><code>../Book-KCNA/sources/k8s-docs-pod-qos-2026-08-24.md</code></summary>\n","numLines":85,"startLine":210,"totalLines":555}}}
